00:04:33.014 --> 00:04:33.930
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:36.240
So we have you here
today because we

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:39.000
wanted to discuss a little
bit about TensorFlow.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.270
But before we start
talking about TensorFlow,

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:45.600
for all the audience that
have never done any machine

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:50.190
learning, what is-- TensorFlow
I've heard is neural networks.

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:51.744
What is a neural network?

00:04:51.744 --> 00:04:52.410
ELI BIXBY: Yeah.

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:55.905
So TensorFlow is mainly
for neural networks.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:02.100
A neural network
in general is-- you

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:05.600
can think of it sort of like
it's a directed graph where

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:09.830
the nodes are simply
like arithmetic

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:12.400
activations of their inputs.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:14.840
So there are some
nodes upstream.

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:17.320
They feed into a
downstream node.

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:22.150
And the node has a single output
based on the upstream nodes.

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:25.360
And it was sort of
discovered a long time ago,

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:28.420
like in the '70s, that
you could use these.

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:30.960
And you could use back
propagation, which basically

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:36.200
says, I want the top layer of
nodes to have this certain--

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:37.840
or I want the
output layer to have

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:41.250
a certain output when the top
layer has a certain input.

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:44.750
And you can use back propagation
to adjust the function

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:50.500
that each node operates on to
line up with these outputs,

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:52.690
and that you can model
very complex functions

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:56.089
with many layers
of neural networks.

00:05:56.089 --> 00:05:57.880
MARK MANDEL: Where did
this idea come from?

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:01.210
Do you know any of the history
of how it sort of came about?

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:02.210
ELI BIXBY: I'm not sure.

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:05.890
I think it was
actually originally

00:06:05.890 --> 00:06:08.650
sort of a theoretical
attempt to model

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:14.810
like human cognition, which
is why it's neural networks.

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:20.420
I'm not sure that that
is part of its usage now.

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:22.954
But I think that's where
the name came from.

00:06:22.954 --> 00:06:23.870
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:26.617
So just to try to
understand a bit better what

00:06:26.617 --> 00:06:29.200
neural networks are-- basically
at the end of the day what you

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:31.220
have is a bunch of
nodes, and every node

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:33.584
is like a function
where you get bunch

00:06:33.584 --> 00:06:34.750
of parameters and an output?

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:35.416
ELI BIXBY: Yeah.

00:06:35.416 --> 00:06:38.719
And the nodes are actually
really a helpful abstraction

00:06:38.719 --> 00:06:39.760
for us to think about it.

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:43.810
But it's actually TensorFlow
is named TensorFlow

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:46.020
because its tensor is
flowing along a graph.

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:49.330
And really you think
of it as the layers

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:54.040
are single entities that are
tensor transformations from one

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:56.460
to the next layer.

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:59.850
And so often we talk about
layers and the activation

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:03.210
function of a layer
of the tensor graph

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:04.784
rather than individual nodes.

00:07:04.784 --> 00:07:05.700
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:11.740
So now we know what TensorFlow
comes from-- the name.

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:13.464
Why is TensorFlow a thing?

00:07:13.464 --> 00:07:14.130
ELI BIXBY: Yeah.

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:21.240
So TensorFlow is the successor
to Google's previous machine

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:22.920
learning framework.

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:25.900
And really the reason
it's open source

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:30.940
is we believe that there is
going to be a lot of innovation

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:31.860
in machine learning.

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:35.550
And we think it's
important for these tools

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:38.730
to sort of be
standardized and for work

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:41.520
to be shared among the
machine learning community.

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:46.504
And so having a common
framework for expressing machine

00:07:46.504 --> 00:07:48.170
learning algorithms
is really important.

00:07:48.170 --> 00:07:50.670
So previously you'd see papers,
and you'd see people kind of

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:53.810
informally describe
the algorithm

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:55.520
that they were writing about.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:58.010
But now you can actually
publish the code.

00:07:58.010 --> 00:08:00.870
And it's another step towards
reproducible research.

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:03.440
It allows people to
collaborate more easily--

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:05.956
all the great things we
love about open source.

00:08:05.956 --> 00:08:07.330
MARK MANDEL: So
you touched on it

00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:09.430
a little bit when you started
talking about tensors flowing

00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:12.160
through a graph, but can we
break that down even further?

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:13.250
What's a tensor?

00:08:13.250 --> 00:08:14.420
How does this work?

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:16.490
Give us sort of like a
base level understanding.

00:08:16.490 --> 00:08:16.750
ELI BIXBY: Yeah.

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:19.208
So mathematicians are going to
hate me for this definition,

00:08:19.208 --> 00:08:24.330
but a tensor is a
multi-dimensional matrix.

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:27.040
So you think of a vector as
just a list of numbers, right?

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:32.200
So like an array in
Python and a matrix

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:34.530
then is a list of
lists of numbers.

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:39.520
And so a tensor is-- a vector
is a one-dimensional tensor.

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:41.799
A matrix is a
two-dimensional tensor.

00:08:41.799 --> 00:08:46.150
And then a matrix of vectors
is a three-dimensional tensor.

00:08:46.150 --> 00:08:50.670
And you can expand that,
and you get this concept

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:54.300
of shape, which is a
vector that represents

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:56.240
the shape of a tensor.

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:58.700
And so for the users who
have used NumPy before,

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:02.710
this should be a very
familiar concept.

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:05.480
TensorFlow actually follows
the exact same syntax

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:08.710
that NumPy does for
defining these things.

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:12.340
MARK MANDEL: And so how
does that correlate into how

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:13.390
we do machine learning?

00:09:13.390 --> 00:09:14.610
Why is that important?

00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:15.620
ELI BIXBY: Yeah.

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:19.215
So it's really important in
particular for deep learning,

00:09:19.215 --> 00:09:20.590
because a lot of
these algorithms

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:26.070
can be thought of as a series
of tensor computations.

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:28.730
And there's a lot
of optimization.

00:09:28.730 --> 00:09:31.860
You can also do restricting
the data that's flowing along

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:32.960
your graph to tensors.

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:35.720
So if you compare it with
something like Apache Beam

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:38.280
or Spark, where there's
sort of arbitrary

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:40.440
data flowing along
your computation graph,

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:42.160
and you can define
your own serialization

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:43.400
and deserialization.

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:46.390
TensorFlow, by contrast,
is all in memory

00:09:46.390 --> 00:09:52.890
and calls out to very fast C
implementations of these tensor

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:54.690
operations.

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:58.980
And obviously that allows you
to make-- like plug into CUDA

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:01.660
and use GPUs really easily.

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:05.402
So the restricting it to tensors
gets us a lot of functionality.

00:10:05.402 --> 00:10:06.610
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Interesting.

00:10:06.610 --> 00:10:10.550
So you actually mention
GPUs, because I'm

00:10:10.550 --> 00:10:12.730
assuming that GPUs
are better suited

00:10:12.730 --> 00:10:14.400
for this kind of computations.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:17.174
But we also announced
this other thing-- TPUs.

00:10:17.174 --> 00:10:17.840
ELI BIXBY: Yeah.

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:20.120
So the strength of GPUs
actually ties a lot

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:24.095
into why we're seeing a
resurgence in neural networks.

00:10:24.095 --> 00:10:26.310
The techniques have been
around for a long time.

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:28.240
And now we have this
commodity hardware

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:31.150
that's just fantastic
for neural networks.

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:34.830
And we're seeing neural
networks be extremely useful

00:10:34.830 --> 00:10:37.760
in all of these applications.

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:39.180
And so we're seeing
a huge growth.

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:42.800
And we're actually
getting to the point

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:46.760
where it makes sense to actually
have ASICs for TensorFlow.

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:48.830
So that's what a TPU is.

00:10:48.830 --> 00:10:50.050
MARK MANDEL: What's an ASIC?

00:10:50.050 --> 00:10:51.095
ELI BIXBY: An ASIC is--

00:10:51.095 --> 00:10:52.220
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Thank you.

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:54.470
ELI BIXBY: I think the acronym
is Application Specific

00:10:54.470 --> 00:10:56.030
Integrated Chip.

00:10:56.030 --> 00:10:59.550
So it's a custom
piece of hardware

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:03.180
that-- and the optimization
you're looking for,

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:05.850
because we have these big
data centers, the optimization

00:11:05.850 --> 00:11:08.110
you're looking for is
performance per watt.

00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:08.610
Right?

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:12.690
So they allow you to
do the operations that

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:17.170
are common in machine
learning at a lower power.

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:19.480
And that means you get
a higher throughput,

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:21.150
because you can run
a lot more of them

00:11:21.150 --> 00:11:23.284
for the same amount
of electricity.

00:11:23.284 --> 00:11:24.200
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:27.960
So it sounds like TensorFlow
is kind of a complicated

00:11:27.960 --> 00:11:30.740
and pretty complex framework.

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:33.110
Does that mean that
to get started with it

00:11:33.110 --> 00:11:36.710
I need to understand what
tensors and monads are?

00:11:36.710 --> 00:11:38.540
ELI BIXBY: No.

00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:42.180
So the actual goal
is that developers

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:44.692
can start writing machine
learning algorithms

00:11:44.692 --> 00:11:46.650
and start incorporating
machine learning easily

00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:51.250
into their code, and that's
a huge goal of TensorFlow.

00:11:51.250 --> 00:11:54.840
So TensorFlow itself, there's
a lot you can do with it.

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:57.672
You can write custom operations.

00:11:57.672 --> 00:11:59.380
You can fiddle around
with the internals.

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:01.722
But we try to
provide a high level

00:12:01.722 --> 00:12:04.180
interface for people who don't
necessarily want to do that.

00:12:04.180 --> 00:12:06.630
And we're actually looking
at building more layers

00:12:06.630 --> 00:12:10.730
on top of TensorFlow to make
it easier for developers

00:12:10.730 --> 00:12:11.770
to get started with it.

00:12:11.770 --> 00:12:16.340
So I think it's
publicly available.

00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:20.730
But I think it's like whatever
the open source version

00:12:20.730 --> 00:12:22.520
of alpha would be considered.

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:25.540
We have a package called
Wide and Deep, which

00:12:25.540 --> 00:12:28.130
provides a higher level
abstraction on top

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:31.890
of TensorFlow that allows you
to say, I have sparse features.

00:12:31.890 --> 00:12:33.970
Learn these sparse features.

00:12:33.970 --> 00:12:36.180
Or like I have dense features.

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:37.550
Learn these dense features.

00:12:37.550 --> 00:12:40.060
And so it's very--
all you really

00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:42.150
have to know about is
what your data looks like.

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:43.070
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:12:43.070 --> 00:12:43.570
Cool.

00:12:43.570 --> 00:12:47.180
So how do I know if
TensorFlow is good for me?

00:12:47.180 --> 00:12:50.690
What are the typical use
cases where the answer

00:12:50.690 --> 00:12:51.960
is like, oh, yeah, TensorFlow.

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:52.626
ELI BIXBY: Yeah.

00:12:52.626 --> 00:12:58.349
So we hope to expand it to a lot
of different machine learning

00:12:58.349 --> 00:12:58.890
applications.

00:12:58.890 --> 00:13:02.510
I think in particular if
you have a problem that's

00:13:02.510 --> 00:13:06.050
well-suited to deep
learning, TensorFlow

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:10.060
is a great solution for you.

00:13:10.060 --> 00:13:11.630
Again, deep learning
algorithms, they

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:14.210
require a huge amount
of data to train,

00:13:14.210 --> 00:13:17.530
more so than many other
machine learning algorithms.

00:13:17.530 --> 00:13:20.110
So you need to have that
at least somewhat available

00:13:20.110 --> 00:13:20.610
to you.

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:22.110
MARK MANDEL: What's
a good heuristic

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:23.624
for a large amount of data?

00:13:23.624 --> 00:13:24.290
ELI BIXBY: Yeah.

00:13:26.890 --> 00:13:30.970
I think sort of the ballpark
lower level is maybe

00:13:30.970 --> 00:13:32.824
like 100,000.

00:13:32.824 --> 00:13:35.060
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:13:35.060 --> 00:13:39.830
ELI BIXBY: Labeled is
common, for example,

00:13:39.830 --> 00:13:45.184
for text-- text or image.

00:13:45.184 --> 00:13:46.100
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:13:46.100 --> 00:13:48.730
So you mentioned this label.

00:13:48.730 --> 00:13:51.770
Does that mean that your
data needs to be labeled?

00:13:51.770 --> 00:13:54.056
And actually, what does
it mean to be labeled?

00:13:54.056 --> 00:13:54.930
And do you need that?

00:13:54.930 --> 00:13:59.270
ELI BIXBY: So we
commonly break down usage

00:13:59.270 --> 00:14:03.200
of deep learning into two
different parts-- training

00:14:03.200 --> 00:14:04.370
and inference.

00:14:04.370 --> 00:14:10.270
And so training is running
this TensorFlow graph

00:14:10.270 --> 00:14:16.490
on your label data and using it
to generate the proper weights.

00:14:16.490 --> 00:14:19.230
And then inferences-- you
have this unlabeled data where

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:22.660
you want to predict the label.

00:14:22.660 --> 00:14:27.360
And so you're giving that to
the trained TensorFlow graph,

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:30.592
and it's spinning out a
probability for the label.

00:14:30.592 --> 00:14:32.050
MARK MANDEL: So
when you say label,

00:14:32.050 --> 00:14:34.425
it's kind of like, all right,
so I've got a bunch of data

00:14:34.425 --> 00:14:36.135
wherein-- I don't know.

00:14:36.135 --> 00:14:36.802
Pick an example.

00:14:36.802 --> 00:14:38.634
FRANCESC CAMPOY: You
have a bunch of emails,

00:14:38.634 --> 00:14:40.460
and you need to know
if it's spam or not.

00:14:40.460 --> 00:14:40.610
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:14:40.610 --> 00:14:41.109
That works.

00:14:41.109 --> 00:14:42.060
That's great.

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:43.710
So you know whether it's spam.

00:14:43.710 --> 00:14:45.390
And so you can feed that
through the system and be like,

00:14:45.390 --> 00:14:46.010
yes, these are spam.

00:14:46.010 --> 00:14:46.540
These aren't.

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:47.385
And it can be like, yay!

00:14:47.385 --> 00:14:48.093
Now I'm learning.

00:14:48.093 --> 00:14:50.180
And then at the end when
you do your inference,

00:14:50.180 --> 00:14:52.240
you can hand it an email and
it will tell you whether or not

00:14:52.240 --> 00:14:54.470
it's spam to, I guess,
some degree of certainty.

00:14:54.470 --> 00:14:55.136
ELI BIXBY: Yeah.

00:14:55.136 --> 00:14:56.970
Email's a perfect
example, because then you

00:14:56.970 --> 00:14:59.210
have a bunch of users
essentially providing you

00:14:59.210 --> 00:14:59.830
with labels.

00:14:59.830 --> 00:15:02.450
You know, they click the
Report Spam button and bam!

00:15:02.450 --> 00:15:07.510
You have another
labeled piece of data.

00:15:07.510 --> 00:15:11.520
And there's increasing
levels of sophistication.

00:15:11.520 --> 00:15:14.500
But that's sort of the basics
is like, I have this label.

00:15:14.500 --> 00:15:17.340
I want to predict the
label of something else.

00:15:17.340 --> 00:15:19.610
So maybe you have like
a confidence cut-off.

00:15:19.610 --> 00:15:21.650
And you're like,
oh, my model is not

00:15:21.650 --> 00:15:23.450
very confident about
this piece of spam,

00:15:23.450 --> 00:15:25.100
so I'm going to
surface it to the user.

00:15:25.100 --> 00:15:26.016
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:15:26.016 --> 00:15:28.140
You were mentioning
something before which

00:15:28.140 --> 00:15:29.390
I thought it was very cool.

00:15:29.390 --> 00:15:32.304
It was the one with
Reddit subreddits.

00:15:32.304 --> 00:15:32.970
ELI BIXBY: Yeah.

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:37.730
So we gave a talk
at OSCON and PyCon

00:15:37.730 --> 00:15:41.470
where we made a little
example algorithm

00:15:41.470 --> 00:15:45.870
based on some colleague's
work with text classification.

00:15:45.870 --> 00:15:50.760
And we used it to classify
Reddit post titles

00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:52.300
by subreddit.

00:15:52.300 --> 00:15:54.460
And we got, I think, a
pretty good accuracy.

00:15:54.460 --> 00:15:57.670
Not state of the art, but
I think it was mid-90's.

00:16:00.590 --> 00:16:05.200
And yeah, these are topics
that are very well-suited,

00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:07.640
because there are large
data sets available.

00:16:07.640 --> 00:16:11.650
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So let's talk
about that specific example.

00:16:11.650 --> 00:16:15.530
How does it look for a
developer to do that?

00:16:15.530 --> 00:16:17.240
I imagine they
have all the titles

00:16:17.240 --> 00:16:19.690
and what subreddit
they belong to.

00:16:19.690 --> 00:16:20.780
What is the next step?

00:16:20.780 --> 00:16:21.446
ELI BIXBY: Yeah.

00:16:21.446 --> 00:16:24.910
So there's going to be like
a pre-processing setup.

00:16:24.910 --> 00:16:31.140
And we frequently use Apache
Beam to preprocess our data.

00:16:31.140 --> 00:16:32.880
And so then once
you have your data

00:16:32.880 --> 00:16:37.510
in sort of this
nice vector format,

00:16:37.510 --> 00:16:42.240
you can go and grab and try
to adapt for your use case

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:43.930
one of the many open sources.

00:16:43.930 --> 00:16:46.420
There are many models
already out there.

00:16:46.420 --> 00:16:49.780
So frequently you'll try to
figure out-- what category

00:16:49.780 --> 00:16:51.110
does my problem fit into?

00:16:51.110 --> 00:16:54.110
OK, it's a text
classification problem.

00:16:54.110 --> 00:16:54.610
Oh, great.

00:16:54.610 --> 00:16:58.790
Someone's already written a
text classification model.

00:16:58.790 --> 00:17:01.790
And then you'll try training
that model on your data

00:17:01.790 --> 00:17:03.640
with your preprocessed data.

00:17:03.640 --> 00:17:06.270
And then you'll do some
testing and some validation

00:17:06.270 --> 00:17:06.890
and say, OK.

00:17:06.890 --> 00:17:07.390
This worked.

00:17:07.390 --> 00:17:08.109
This didn't work.

00:17:08.109 --> 00:17:10.910
Let me tinker around with
the model a little bit

00:17:10.910 --> 00:17:13.599
until you get sort of an
accuracy you're happy with.

00:17:13.599 --> 00:17:14.930
MARK MANDEL: Let's sort of
take even a further step back.

00:17:14.930 --> 00:17:15.429
OK.

00:17:15.429 --> 00:17:16.950
So you say model.

00:17:16.950 --> 00:17:18.700
What does that input
and output look like?

00:17:18.700 --> 00:17:19.334
What's a model?

00:17:19.334 --> 00:17:20.500
How does that work together?

00:17:20.500 --> 00:17:21.958
ELI BIXBY: So
TensorFlow graphs are

00:17:21.958 --> 00:17:23.390
defined with this Python code.

00:17:23.390 --> 00:17:26.220
So TensorFlow is like
a Python Package.

00:17:26.220 --> 00:17:28.590
You write sort of operations.

00:17:28.590 --> 00:17:31.590
And an operation
will typically have

00:17:31.590 --> 00:17:33.970
arguments that are previous
operations you've defined.

00:17:33.970 --> 00:17:37.840
So in this way you're defining
like a directed graph.

00:17:37.840 --> 00:17:39.652
So there's two parts.

00:17:39.652 --> 00:17:40.860
There's the graph definition.

00:17:40.860 --> 00:17:42.630
And then there's
the training step

00:17:42.630 --> 00:17:45.930
where you're running through
basically a loop saying, OK.

00:17:45.930 --> 00:17:49.250
Grab a batch of
my training data.

00:17:49.250 --> 00:17:51.180
Run it through the graph.

00:17:51.180 --> 00:17:54.390
It'll calculate gradients.

00:17:54.390 --> 00:17:57.610
It will update the
operations on each node,

00:17:57.610 --> 00:18:00.050
like you were talking about.

00:18:00.050 --> 00:18:02.939
And then do that
until we're satisfied

00:18:02.939 --> 00:18:04.480
with whatever accuracy
we're getting,

00:18:04.480 --> 00:18:06.070
or whatever loss we're getting.

00:18:06.070 --> 00:18:10.970
Whatever function we've
decided is our metric.

00:18:10.970 --> 00:18:13.620
And then that will
constitute a model.

00:18:13.620 --> 00:18:16.541
And then there are also lots
of sort of ops to your packages

00:18:16.541 --> 00:18:17.540
that we've open sourced.

00:18:17.540 --> 00:18:19.560
So for example,
TensorFlow serving

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:23.300
allows you to serve
models who do inference.

00:18:23.300 --> 00:18:25.280
That's the other half.

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:28.770
So you'll export a model, which
is exporting these weights

00:18:28.770 --> 00:18:30.930
and this graph to a file.

00:18:30.930 --> 00:18:33.700
And then TensorFlow Serving
can load it for you,

00:18:33.700 --> 00:18:39.070
and then provide you an
API-- a gRPC API-- where

00:18:39.070 --> 00:18:42.290
you can call it with a vector.

00:18:42.290 --> 00:18:44.290
And it'll return back
the output vector

00:18:44.290 --> 00:18:46.765
that you'd expect,
maybe the labels.

00:18:46.765 --> 00:18:49.140
MARK MANDEL: So if I was taking
say, like a Reddit title,

00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:51.550
and I'm going to convert
that into like a vector.

00:18:51.550 --> 00:18:53.060
What does that vector look like?

00:18:53.060 --> 00:18:55.390
ELI BIXBY: Yeah.

00:18:55.390 --> 00:18:58.460
So I talked a little bit before,
without explaining myself,

00:18:58.460 --> 00:19:02.600
about sparse data
and dense data.

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:06.440
So like Reddit,
maybe a Reddit title

00:19:06.440 --> 00:19:10.720
is a list of vectors
where each vector is

00:19:10.720 --> 00:19:13.200
your entire vocabulary.

00:19:13.200 --> 00:19:15.650
So maybe like 40,000 words
in the English language

00:19:15.650 --> 00:19:17.160
or whatever.

00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:20.770
And there's a 1 somewhere
in there corresponding

00:19:20.770 --> 00:19:21.270
to the word.

00:19:21.270 --> 00:19:23.850
Like cat is a 1.

00:19:23.850 --> 00:19:27.750
And maybe cat is like
the 30,250th word.

00:19:27.750 --> 00:19:31.170
So like the 30,250th index
in your vector is a 1.

00:19:31.170 --> 00:19:32.860
And everything else is 0's.

00:19:32.860 --> 00:19:36.180
So that's sparse data.

00:19:36.180 --> 00:19:40.940
Frequently in deep learning
we'll do embeddings.

00:19:40.940 --> 00:19:46.400
So we'll try to learn denser
representations of data,

00:19:46.400 --> 00:19:49.250
because the neural nets
tend to handle these denser

00:19:49.250 --> 00:19:50.500
representations better.

00:19:53.980 --> 00:19:58.000
And this is something we
talked about in our workshop,

00:19:58.000 --> 00:20:00.200
words actually lend
themselves very

00:20:00.200 --> 00:20:02.630
well to this dense embedding.

00:20:02.630 --> 00:20:05.220
So you may actually
already have a model.

00:20:05.220 --> 00:20:08.060
So there may be-- if
you look at-- so we open

00:20:08.060 --> 00:20:09.930
sourced SyntaxNet,
for example, which

00:20:09.930 --> 00:20:14.670
is a piece of Google's natural
language processing written

00:20:14.670 --> 00:20:15.640
in TensorFlow.

00:20:15.640 --> 00:20:17.720
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Wasn't
it called Parsey McFarsi?

00:20:17.720 --> 00:20:18.386
ELI BIXBY: Yeah.

00:20:18.386 --> 00:20:19.980
So there's a subtle
difference here.

00:20:19.980 --> 00:20:21.600
We open sourced two things.

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:25.380
We open sourced SyntaxNet,
which is the graph definition.

00:20:25.380 --> 00:20:27.540
And then we open
sourced a trained model.

00:20:27.540 --> 00:20:32.720
So including the weights
and all the trained bits,

00:20:32.720 --> 00:20:34.280
called Parsey
McParseface, which was

00:20:34.280 --> 00:20:37.400
trained on a subset of
the data that we have.

00:20:37.400 --> 00:20:39.360
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:20:39.360 --> 00:20:41.610
ELI BIXBY: And so if
you look at SyntaxNet,

00:20:41.610 --> 00:20:45.360
it's actually a bunch
of trained pieces,

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:48.170
a bunch of different neural
nets that feed into each other.

00:20:48.170 --> 00:20:52.654
So there's this sense
of composability, which

00:20:52.654 --> 00:20:53.820
brings me back to embedding.

00:20:53.820 --> 00:20:56.290
So you may have a model that
somebody else has already

00:20:56.290 --> 00:20:59.550
written for generating
word embeddings where you

00:20:59.550 --> 00:21:02.420
feed in your one hot vector.

00:21:02.420 --> 00:21:05.220
And it gives you back a denser
representation of words.

00:21:05.220 --> 00:21:08.080
So maybe what you're
learning is a mapping

00:21:08.080 --> 00:21:12.370
from these word embeddings
to subreddit classification.

00:21:12.370 --> 00:21:17.780
And so that's part of what we
hope to spur by this ecosystem

00:21:17.780 --> 00:21:21.850
is like this sense of
composability and other people

00:21:21.850 --> 00:21:24.270
taking their models
and using them

00:21:24.270 --> 00:21:28.000
as pieces of their own
models, and then providing

00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:29.750
those models as pieces
for other people

00:21:29.750 --> 00:21:34.124
to use, and just sort of
building this whole ecosystem.

00:21:34.124 --> 00:21:35.040
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:21:35.040 --> 00:21:39.560
So we've been talking about
models and that basically

00:21:39.560 --> 00:21:42.564
you're going to be training
some neural network where

00:21:42.564 --> 00:21:43.480
you create that model.

00:21:43.480 --> 00:21:45.870
And then that model is a
valuable piece of information

00:21:45.870 --> 00:21:47.420
that you can use.

00:21:47.420 --> 00:21:49.839
How big is a model?

00:21:49.839 --> 00:21:50.630
ELI BIXBY: Oh, man.

00:21:50.630 --> 00:21:52.270
I think it varies a huge amount.

00:21:52.270 --> 00:21:56.020
I'm not even sure how well
I can give a ballpark.

00:21:56.020 --> 00:22:00.050
I think it depends a huge
amount on the variables,

00:22:00.050 --> 00:22:00.840
like the size.

00:22:00.840 --> 00:22:05.344
Like if we had a
vocabulary of 50 words,

00:22:05.344 --> 00:22:07.510
and we were doing-- or not
a vocabulary of 50 words.

00:22:07.510 --> 00:22:11.390
If we were doing an embedding
in like a vector of 50 link,

00:22:11.390 --> 00:22:13.730
that's going to be a much
smaller file than an embedding

00:22:13.730 --> 00:22:16.292
with a vector of 100 link.

00:22:16.292 --> 00:22:18.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Are we
talking gigabytes here?

00:22:18.250 --> 00:22:18.750
Terabytes?

00:22:18.750 --> 00:22:21.080
ELI BIXBY: I think we
can be talking gigabytes.

00:22:21.080 --> 00:22:23.200
Smaller models, I think,
may be under that.

00:22:23.200 --> 00:22:27.047
But definitely I think
probably not a terabyte.

00:22:27.047 --> 00:22:27.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:22:27.880 --> 00:22:28.490
So OK.

00:22:28.490 --> 00:22:29.719
So it's going to be--

00:22:29.719 --> 00:22:31.260
ELI BIXBY: On the
order of gigabytes.

00:22:31.260 --> 00:22:31.560
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:22:31.560 --> 00:22:32.490
Cool.

00:22:32.490 --> 00:22:35.115
MARK MANDEL: So if I want to sit
down, and I'm like, all right.

00:22:35.115 --> 00:22:37.420
This thing sounds super cool,
sounds super interesting.

00:22:37.420 --> 00:22:38.910
How would I get started
with TensorFlow?

00:22:38.910 --> 00:22:40.100
Where is a good place to start?

00:22:40.100 --> 00:22:40.270
ELI BIXBY: Yeah.

00:22:40.270 --> 00:22:42.730
So TensorFlow.org has a
bunch of great tutorials

00:22:42.730 --> 00:22:45.085
that the Brain
Team has provided.

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:51.580
MNIST is kind of the
Hello World of TensorFlow.

00:22:51.580 --> 00:22:53.320
So that's this problem
that's been around

00:22:53.320 --> 00:22:56.150
for a long time where you
have handwritten digits,

00:22:56.150 --> 00:22:59.980
and you want to classify
them to the correct number.

00:22:59.980 --> 00:23:03.520
And so I think that's sort of
the first place to get started.

00:23:03.520 --> 00:23:05.680
And there's an
MNIST for beginners.

00:23:05.680 --> 00:23:08.030
And there's an MNIST
for ML experts.

00:23:08.030 --> 00:23:11.950
So you know, it'll walk
you through what is MNIST.

00:23:11.950 --> 00:23:13.550
Why is it important?

00:23:13.550 --> 00:23:15.370
Here's a little bit of
the math behind sort

00:23:15.370 --> 00:23:19.910
of the state-of-the-art
MNIST algorithms,

00:23:19.910 --> 00:23:21.190
if you're a beginner.

00:23:21.190 --> 00:23:23.550
And if not, it will be just
like, here's TensorFlow.

00:23:23.550 --> 00:23:26.420
And here's how it corresponds
to the MNIST algorithm

00:23:26.420 --> 00:23:27.860
that you're familiar with.

00:23:27.860 --> 00:23:28.985
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:23:28.985 --> 00:23:31.200
Also, since you
mentioned that workshop

00:23:31.200 --> 00:23:33.540
that you gave at
OSCON and PyCon.

00:23:33.540 --> 00:23:35.420
That was like three
hours, and it is online.

00:23:35.420 --> 00:23:37.420
Could you recommend
that as an entry point

00:23:37.420 --> 00:23:39.170
to someone that has
never done TensorFlow?

00:23:39.170 --> 00:23:39.887
ELI BIXBY: Yeah.

00:23:39.887 --> 00:23:41.470
We tried to make it
pretty accessible.

00:23:41.470 --> 00:23:43.530
I think even three
hours, it's kind of

00:23:43.530 --> 00:23:47.580
hard to fit in a
comprehensive intro.

00:23:47.580 --> 00:23:50.930
And so I think it's
a little squashed.

00:23:50.930 --> 00:23:53.550
But certainly you
should check that out.

00:23:53.550 --> 00:23:56.490
And even if it's just
for the resource slides,

00:23:56.490 --> 00:23:58.260
we have a lot of
resource slides in there

00:23:58.260 --> 00:24:01.104
to sort of get you
started on your own.

00:24:01.104 --> 00:24:02.020
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:24:02.020 --> 00:24:03.240
That sounds very interesting.

00:24:03.240 --> 00:24:05.220
We're kind of
running out of time.

00:24:05.220 --> 00:24:06.970
Is there anything else
that you would like

00:24:06.970 --> 00:24:09.350
to mention before we finish?

00:24:09.350 --> 00:24:11.260
ELI BIXBY: Not off
the top of my head.

00:24:11.260 --> 00:24:13.600
Definitely check out TensorFlow.

00:24:13.600 --> 00:24:16.930
It's worth a look.

00:24:16.930 --> 00:24:19.020
FRANCESC CAMPOY: One
last question, actually.

00:24:19.020 --> 00:24:22.587
What is the best use case
of TensorFlow, the most fun

00:24:22.587 --> 00:24:25.510
usage of TensorFlow
you have ever seen?

00:24:25.510 --> 00:24:30.940
ELI BIXBY: So I love DCGANs
which is some crazy acronym.

00:24:30.940 --> 00:24:34.910
But Generative Adversarial
Network is the last bit.

00:24:34.910 --> 00:24:37.320
So it's actually two
neural nets that are

00:24:37.320 --> 00:24:39.100
competing against each other.

00:24:39.100 --> 00:24:41.860
One of them is
trying to classify

00:24:41.860 --> 00:24:44.600
the output of the first
one and your training data.

00:24:44.600 --> 00:24:47.670
And the other one is trying to
generate from noise things that

00:24:47.670 --> 00:24:49.131
look like your training data.

00:24:49.131 --> 00:24:50.880
And so you're training
this neural network

00:24:50.880 --> 00:24:54.660
to generate-- to trick
this other neural network,

00:24:54.660 --> 00:24:55.160
basically.

00:24:55.160 --> 00:24:56.201
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, wow.

00:24:56.201 --> 00:24:58.240
ELI BIXBY: And so
there's a bunch

00:24:58.240 --> 00:25:02.140
of hilarious
pictures in the paper

00:25:02.140 --> 00:25:06.390
and in the git hub of
like, generated bedrooms.

00:25:06.390 --> 00:25:08.590
And they're like
uncanny valley bedrooms.

00:25:08.590 --> 00:25:10.837
They're things that look
almost like bedrooms,

00:25:10.837 --> 00:25:12.503
but there is something
wrong about them.

00:25:12.503 --> 00:25:13.253
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:25:13.253 --> 00:25:16.260
ELI BIXBY: And there's
generated faces.

00:25:16.260 --> 00:25:17.900
And they're like
uncanny valley faces.

00:25:17.900 --> 00:25:19.697
It's like they're almost faces.

00:25:19.697 --> 00:25:20.530
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:25:20.530 --> 00:25:21.905
We're going to
add a link to that

00:25:21.905 --> 00:25:23.124
and definitely check it out.

00:25:23.124 --> 00:25:23.915
It sounds very fun.

00:25:23.915 --> 00:25:24.890
ELI BIXBY: Absolutely.

00:25:24.890 --> 00:25:25.860
MARK MANDEL: Well,
