00:25:26.940 --> 00:25:28.300
We really appreciate
you taking the time.

00:25:28.300 --> 00:25:28.490
ELI BIXBY: Yeah.

00:25:28.490 --> 00:25:29.365
Thanks for having me.

00:25:29.365 --> 00:25:30.540
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Thank you.

00:25:30.540 --> 00:25:34.240
So thank you to Eli for such
an interesting conversation.

00:25:34.240 --> 00:25:36.010
Before we move to
the next topic,

00:25:36.010 --> 00:25:38.290
I wanted to add a
couple of corrections

00:25:38.290 --> 00:25:42.520
that he actually gave as
soon as we stopped recording.

00:25:42.520 --> 00:25:48.350
One of them-- we asked him
how big TensorFlow models are,

00:25:48.350 --> 00:25:51.940
and he said probably
around gigabytes.

00:25:51.940 --> 00:25:54.726
And it turns out it's
more like megabytes.

00:25:54.726 --> 00:25:56.600
And he said that the
problem is that he's not

00:25:56.600 --> 00:25:58.130
used to counting
that low anymore,

00:25:58.130 --> 00:26:00.040
since he works at Google.

00:26:00.040 --> 00:26:01.280
Kind of humble, right?

00:26:01.280 --> 00:26:03.090
But yeah, it's
actually megabytes.

00:26:03.090 --> 00:26:06.470
So you can consider sending
them to your mobile phone

00:26:06.470 --> 00:26:09.680
or having them as part of your
app, which is pretty cool.

00:26:09.680 --> 00:26:12.030
And then something that
is not a correction,

00:26:12.030 --> 00:26:15.120
but I wanted to add anyway.

00:26:15.120 --> 00:26:17.900
He mentioned Apache
Beam, which is actually

00:26:17.900 --> 00:26:19.870
something you can run on
Google Cloud Platform,

00:26:19.870 --> 00:26:25.810
on Google Cloud Dataflow, and
also TensorFlow you can run it.

00:26:25.810 --> 00:26:27.280
Or you'll be able
to run it soon,

00:26:27.280 --> 00:26:30.880
because it's limited preview
for now on Google Cloud ML.

00:26:30.880 --> 00:26:33.400
ML stands for Machine
Learning, obviously.

00:26:33.400 --> 00:26:34.520
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:26:34.520 --> 00:26:35.436
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:26:37.390 --> 00:26:39.350
So let's go to the
question of the week.

00:26:39.350 --> 00:26:42.610
And I think it comes from-- you
got the question of the week.

00:26:42.610 --> 00:26:44.610
MARK MANDEL: I did get
the question of the week.

00:26:44.610 --> 00:26:48.030
It was from some people I
was chatting with this week.

00:26:48.030 --> 00:26:50.740
And they were talking
about how-- OK.

00:26:50.740 --> 00:26:55.170
Say you have 10 terabytes, 15
terabytes, 20 terabytes of data

00:26:55.170 --> 00:26:57.910
sort of lying around, maybe on
a nest, maybe on a hard drive,

00:26:57.910 --> 00:27:00.200
maybe running on-prem,
something like that.

00:27:00.200 --> 00:27:02.980
And you want to move everything
over to Google Cloud Platform.

00:27:02.980 --> 00:27:04.830
And you're like,
this is going to take

00:27:04.830 --> 00:27:06.840
me a really long
time to upload if I

00:27:06.840 --> 00:27:08.630
have to do it over the wire.

00:27:08.630 --> 00:27:10.144
So what do I do?

00:27:10.144 --> 00:27:11.810
FRANCESC CAMPOY: You
encode it into XML.

00:27:11.810 --> 00:27:13.123
And then you-- no.

00:27:13.123 --> 00:27:15.210
No, no, no.

00:27:15.210 --> 00:27:17.310
So I mean, there's
one option, which

00:27:17.310 --> 00:27:21.510
is you can use gsutil
to start sending

00:27:21.510 --> 00:27:24.650
that file through the network.

00:27:24.650 --> 00:27:26.400
And that's going to
take a very long time.

00:27:26.400 --> 00:27:27.150
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:27:27.150 --> 00:27:27.890
Weeks, months.

00:27:27.890 --> 00:27:28.230
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:27:28.230 --> 00:27:29.760
Like long, long, long time.

00:27:29.760 --> 00:27:32.300
So instead of doing that,
there's a couple options.

00:27:32.300 --> 00:27:34.900
And one of them was the
one that you mentioned.

00:27:34.900 --> 00:27:35.650
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:27:35.650 --> 00:27:38.630
So we have a couple of
partners, one of which

00:27:38.630 --> 00:27:42.070
is Iron Mountain for US areas.

00:27:42.070 --> 00:27:46.500
And the other one is Prime Focus
Technologies for EMEA and APAC.

00:27:46.500 --> 00:27:47.670
Pretty much is-- yeah.

00:27:47.670 --> 00:27:49.440
You can take a hard
drive, bundle it up,

00:27:49.440 --> 00:27:52.140
stick it on a courier
like FedEx or UPS,

00:27:52.140 --> 00:27:53.880
and send it directly to them.

00:27:53.880 --> 00:27:57.037
And they'll load it up
for you quick smart.

00:27:57.037 --> 00:27:58.870
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
that is actually-- yeah.

00:27:58.870 --> 00:28:03.890
I think that Eli mentioned
that, that there's

00:28:03.890 --> 00:28:05.640
no wider then-- no.

00:28:05.640 --> 00:28:06.471
How do you say it?

00:28:06.471 --> 00:28:07.220
MARK MANDEL: What?

00:28:07.220 --> 00:28:09.549
FRANCESC CAMPOY: The
band is bandwidth.

00:28:09.549 --> 00:28:10.090
There you go.

00:28:10.090 --> 00:28:11.687
MARK MANDEL: There we go.

00:28:11.687 --> 00:28:13.770
FRANCESC CAMPOY: There's
no connection with higher

00:28:13.770 --> 00:28:15.610
bandwidth than the mail.

00:28:15.610 --> 00:28:16.620
Like post mail.

00:28:16.620 --> 00:28:18.680
You just send it,
and we receive it.

00:28:18.680 --> 00:28:20.590
Well, our partners
receive it and upload it.

00:28:20.590 --> 00:28:23.080
And they appear directly
onto Google Cloud Storage.

00:28:23.080 --> 00:28:25.400
So that is probably one
of the fastest options.

00:28:25.400 --> 00:28:26.920
But I wanted to
add one more, which

00:28:26.920 --> 00:28:30.590
is what if you really want
to make this very fast

00:28:30.590 --> 00:28:32.490
and all the time?

00:28:32.490 --> 00:28:35.340
It's not that you're sending--
you have 10 terabytes,

00:28:35.340 --> 00:28:36.850
and you have to send them once.

00:28:36.850 --> 00:28:40.510
But it's you keep on generating
10 terabytes per day,

00:28:40.510 --> 00:28:44.350
and you want to send that as
soon as possible onto Google

00:28:44.350 --> 00:28:45.180
Cloud.

00:28:45.180 --> 00:28:47.220
And there's a solution for that.

00:28:47.220 --> 00:28:49.610
It's what we call Google
Cloud Interconnect.

00:28:49.610 --> 00:28:52.870
And basically what you
get is rather than having

00:28:52.870 --> 00:28:58.660
public internet connection
from your data center to ours,

00:28:58.660 --> 00:29:05.490
you're going to get very
good quality connection.

00:29:05.490 --> 00:29:08.380
I think they use the term
enterprise grade, which I'm not

00:29:08.380 --> 00:29:10.000
really sure what that means.

00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:12.280
But I'm assuming
it's really fast.

00:29:12.280 --> 00:29:14.750
And it connects directly
from your data center

00:29:14.750 --> 00:29:16.740
to Google's network edge.

00:29:16.740 --> 00:29:19.450
MARK MANDEL: So this would be if
you had a sort of on-prem cloud

00:29:19.450 --> 00:29:20.864
mixup type deal.

00:29:20.864 --> 00:29:21.780
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:29:21.780 --> 00:29:24.980
I could imagine something like
that where for instance, you're

00:29:24.980 --> 00:29:30.210
doing something like very, very
intense data, like big data.

00:29:30.210 --> 00:29:32.150
And you're generating
a huge amount of data,

00:29:32.150 --> 00:29:34.595
and you want to send it directly
to Big Query or something

00:29:34.595 --> 00:29:35.330
like that.

00:29:35.330 --> 00:29:39.020
And in that case, if you have
such a huge amount of data,

00:29:39.020 --> 00:29:42.820
you need to send it somehow and
maybe sending disks via mail

00:29:42.820 --> 00:29:43.930
is not good enough.

00:29:43.930 --> 00:29:47.450
So in that case you can go
through Cloud Interconnect.

00:29:47.450 --> 00:29:50.050
The pricing for
it-- it's actually

00:29:50.050 --> 00:29:51.670
something that
depends on the cases.

00:29:51.670 --> 00:29:54.170
So you need to
contact a provider.

00:29:54.170 --> 00:29:56.720
I guess it depends on how
far away you live from a data

00:29:56.720 --> 00:29:58.264
center and stuff like that.

00:29:58.264 --> 00:29:58.930
MARK MANDEL: No.

00:29:58.930 --> 00:29:59.790
That sounds really cool.

00:29:59.790 --> 00:30:01.331
Maybe you have
requirements that mean

00:30:01.331 --> 00:30:03.770
you have to be on-prem
for various things,

00:30:03.770 --> 00:30:05.020
like your app has to be there.

00:30:05.020 --> 00:30:06.870
But you want to use
their big data tools,

00:30:06.870 --> 00:30:08.494
and you want to send
that data through.

00:30:08.494 --> 00:30:10.830
That sounds really good.

00:30:10.830 --> 00:30:12.210
That's cool.

00:30:12.210 --> 00:30:15.429
The whole sending of hard
drives through UPS or FedEx

00:30:15.429 --> 00:30:17.970
could be like the one time thing
you do if you're moving over

00:30:17.970 --> 00:30:19.870
to the cloud, if you're
on-prem and you want

00:30:19.870 --> 00:30:22.160
to make that shift as well.

00:30:22.160 --> 00:30:23.076
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

